# ── API ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave **commented out** unless you mean to override the per-platform default.
# `src/config.ts` already picks the right host per platform — android gets
# `10.0.2.2` (the emulator's alias for the Mac's loopback), web and iOS get
# `localhost` — and an env var applies to *every* platform at once. Pinning
# `10.0.2.2` here is how web ended up calling an address a browser cannot
# resolve. Set it only for a physical device on the LAN, or a deployed API.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.1.212:4000

# ── Google Maps: Android ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used by the tracking map and the address picker. `app.config.js` injects it
# into `android.config.googleMaps.apiKey`.
#
# **Setting this is not enough on its own.** The value only reaches the native
# project when prebuild runs, and `expo run:android` reuses an existing
# `android/` directory as-is — so after changing this you must:
#
#   npx expo prebuild --clean -p android
#   grep -c "com.google.android.geo.API_KEY" android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml   # expect 1
#
# Skipping that is how the map spent weeks rendering grey tiles with a
# perfectly correct key sitting in this file. Nothing fails loudly — Google
# Maps with no key just draws nothing.
#
# The key also needs **Maps SDK for Android** enabled and billing on the
# project, and — if it is restricted to an Android app — the SHA-1 of whichever
# keystore signed the build. A local debug build is signed with
# `~/.android/debug.keystore`, *not* the EAS certificate, so both fingerprints
# have to be listed under `com.haala.customer`:
#
#   keytool -list -v -keystore ~/.android/debug.keystore -alias androiddebugkey -storepass android
#
# Not needed for iOS: `DeliveryMap` uses `PROVIDER_DEFAULT`, which is Apple
# Maps there. Never works in Expo Go, which ships its own key and ignores
# `app.config.js` — judge maps from a dev build or the APK.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# ── Google Maps: web ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A **separate** key for the Maps JavaScript API, and it has to be separate:
# the Android key above is not `EXPO_PUBLIC_`, so it never reaches the web
# bundle, and an Android-restricted key does not work in a browser anyway.
#
# `EXPO_PUBLIC_` means this one *is* visible in the shipped bundle. That is
# unavoidable and is how Google's browser keys work — so restrict it by **HTTP
# referrer** and enable only **Maps JavaScript API** on it. Unset, the map
# falls back to a neutral panel and every other part of the screen still works.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_WEB_KEY=

# ── Google sign-in ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Client IDs from the Google Cloud Console. **No `EXPO_PUBLIC_` prefix**, and
# that is deliberate: these reach the app through `app.config.js`, which puts
# them in `extra` for `expo-constants` to read — the same route the Maps key
# takes. One variable name then works both locally and as an EAS variable,
# secret or not.
#
# Do **not** rename these to `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` and read them from a component.
# `process.env` in app code only yields what Metro inlined at bundle time, so an
# unprefixed name is `undefined` on a device — and a prefixed one is a second
# name to keep in sync. Either way the mistake is invisible in development,
# which is how it shipped once: the Google button rendered disabled and pressing
# it did nothing.
#
# They are not secrets in any case. `extra` ships inside the APK and is
# extractable from it, exactly as an inlined value would be; what protects them
# is the package + SHA-1 restriction on the Android client, not concealment.
# There is no client secret anywhere in this app.
#
# **`GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_WEB` is the one that matters, on every platform.**
# Android and iOS use Google's native SDK, which takes the *web* client id as
# the audience it mints the ID token for — without one there is no `idToken` at
# all. So a signed-in Android customer produces a token whose `aud` is the web
# client id, and that is what `GOOGLE_OAUTH_AUDIENCES` on the API must contain.
#
# An **Android** OAuth client still has to exist in the console, bound to
# `com.haala.customer` with the SHA-1 of every keystore that signs a build —
# your local debug one *and* the EAS credentials — because that is how Google
# verifies the app's signature. Its id is never read by the code, which is why
# `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_ANDROID` below is optional.
#
# There is no redirect and no custom URI scheme any more: the browser flow was
# replaced because Google deprecated custom schemes on Android, and even
# re-enabled they broke whenever the OS restarted the app behind the browser.
#
# The API must accept whichever ids you use: set `GOOGLE_OAUTH_AUDIENCES` there
# to a comma-separated list of them, or `verify.ts` rejects the token after
# Google has happily issued it.
#
# Unset, the Google button renders disabled with a reason and email sign-in
# still works.
# Required — used by every platform. See above.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_WEB=
# Optional. The Android client must exist in the console, but its id is unused.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_ANDROID=
# Optional, iOS only.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_IOS=
